      // ── Opening sequence ──────────────────────────────────────────
      // The thread doesn't arrive fully written: it plays itself out
      // on load, so the drill-in reads as the agent working rather
      // than as a page that was already finished.
      //
      // Each stage shows the thinking indicator for `thinkingMs`, then
      // reveals the messages it names. Stages run back to back, so the
      // seller sees: thinking → the agent's answer → thinking again →
      // the list and what to do with it.
      //
      //   thinkingMs    — how long this stage's indicator shows, in ms.
      //   thinkingLabel — copy inside it. Falls back to `thinkingLabel`
      //                   in home_conversation.json.
      //   messageIds    — ids from `messages` above, revealed together
      //                   when the stage ends.
      //
      // A message no stage names renders at first paint, above
      // everything the stages bring in — that's the escape hatch for
      // content that shouldn't wait. Remove `opening` entirely and the
      // whole thread paints at once, as it did before.
      "opening": {
        "stages": [
          {
            "id": "open-01",
            "thinkingMs": 2000,
            "thinkingLabel": "Searching your accounts for prospects\u2026",
            "messageIds": ["msg-response"]
          },
          {
            "id": "open-02",
            "thinkingMs": 2800,
            "thinkingLabel": "Preparing personalized outreach\u2026",
            "messageIds": ["msg-prospects", "msg-activity", "msg-next-steps"]
          }
        ]
      },
      // ── Scripted follow-up turns ──────────────────────────────────
      // The demo reply queue. Each send in the composer consumes the
      // NEXT entry: the thinking indicator shows for `thinkingMs`, then
      // `reply` is appended to the thread. Once the queue runs out,
      // later sends still echo the seller's message but no reply comes
      // back, so the demo ends cleanly instead of looping.
      //
      // `reply` entries use the exact same shape as `messages` above
      // and render through the same macros — so a turn can answer with
      // an `agent` message carrying a full prospect `table` or a
      // cadence of drafted touches, not just prose. Ids must be unique
      // across the whole thread (they become `data-sh-conv-msg` hooks).
      //
      //   thinkingMs    — how long the indicator shows, in ms.
      //   thinkingLabel — copy inside the indicator. Falls back to
      //                   `thinkingLabel` in home_conversation.json.
      //   rowsFrom      — on a reply whose component needs a prospect
      //                   roster (the cadence card, or the parked
      //                   outreach review): the id of the message whose
      //                   `table.rows` it should page through. Lets the
      //                   component arrive later than the list it acts
      //                   on without a second copy of the records.
      //
      // The seeded flow: the thread opens on the prospect list, and the
      // seller's first message — whatever they type — brings back the
      // drafted cadence for those same twelve prospects.
      "scriptedTurns": [
        {
          "id": "turn-01",
          "thinkingMs": 2400,
          "thinkingLabel": "Drafting your sequences\u2026",
          "reply": [
            {
              "id":   "turn-01-cadence",
              "type": "agent",
              "text": "Here are the drafted sequences, one per prospect across email, LinkedIn, and call. Step through a prospect's touches below, or approve all 25 at once.",
              "rowsFrom": "msg-prospects",
              // ── Cadence ───────────────────────────────────────────
              // One drafted sequence of touches per prospect, shown one
              // prospect at a time. Selecting a row in the table above
              // (or the card's own pager) switches which sequence shows.
              //
              //   name / summary   — the cadence header. The touch count
              //                      and channel mix are derived from the
              //                      steps, so they can't drift.
              //   sequences        — keyed by PROSPECT ID, with a
